        <body>
            <div xml:id="d70">

                <pb facs="0148" n="100" sameAs="frus:frus1877/pg100"/>

                <head>No. 70.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Seward to Mr. Evarts.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1877-05-12"><hi rendition="#italic">May</hi> 12, 1877</date>.
                        (Received June 29.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 239.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to hand to you herewith a
                    slip cut from the North China Daily News of the 20th ultimo, which reports that
                    the foreigners <pb facs="0149" n="101" xml:id="pg101"/> employed in the Woosung
                    Railway service have been re-engaged for a further period of eighteen months.
                    The railway has not yet been turned over to the Chinese, but it would seem that
                    the latter must have intimated their intention to operate it upon coming into
                    possession and their desire to retain the foreigners mentioned. This result is
                    satisfactory and justifies the statement heretofore made by me that the
                    government do not really regard railroad enterprises with disfavor, although too
                    little in earnest, or too fearful of the consequences of their introduction, to
                    enter upon their construction.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>GEORGE F. SEWARD.</signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d70-a1.xml" xpointer="d70-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
